---
import StandardsLayout from "../../components/StandardsLayout.astro";
import CitationBlock from "../../components/CitationBlock.astro";
import SectionBlock from "../../components/SectionBlock.astro";
import { derivePublicationMetadata } from "../../utils/publication";

const pageTitle = "Meta-critique across standards";
const pageDescription =
  "Ethotechnics’ core critique of prevailing AI governance standards and regimes — and of itself, where the same failures show up in its own style.";
const permalink = "/standards/meta-critique";
const anchorLinks = [
  { href: "#overview", label: "Core critique" },
  { href: "#contrast", label: "Governance by control" },
  { href: "#style", label: "Style as a moat" },
  { href: "#reading-contract", label: "The reading contract" },
];
const publication = derivePublicationMetadata({
  permalink,
  published: "2025-06-01T00:00:00Z",
});
---

<StandardsLayout
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  permalink={permalink}
  seoTitle={`${pageTitle} — Standards critique`}
  eyebrow="Meta-critique"
  anchorLinks={anchorLinks}
  publishedTime="2025-06-01T00:00:00.000Z"
>
  <SectionBlock
    id="overview"
    eyebrow="Core critique"
    title="Governance by representation isn’t enough"
    description="Most standards govern via documents, roles, reviews, and attestations."
  >
    <p>
      Ethotechnics’ critique isn’t that prevailing standards are “bad.” It’s
      that they mostly govern by representation: documents, roles, reviews, and
      attestations. Modern harm requires governance by control.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="contrast"
    eyebrow="Governance by control"
    title="What Ethotechnics insists on"
    variant="alt"
  >
    <ul class="card__list">
      <li>Stop rules that define explicit triggers and authority.</li>
      <li>
        Binding clocks with escalation paths and measurable recovery time.
      </li>
      <li>Rollback authority and restoration performance under stress.</li>
      <li>Contestability that changes state, not just narrative.</li>
    </ul>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="style"
    eyebrow="Self-critique"
    title="Style as a moat"
    description="The project’s own idiom can become the same procedural filter it critiques."
  >
    <p>
      Ethotechnics writes in a compressed, structural idiom, and the compression
      is doing work: it keeps the claims precise and makes them hard to quote
      loosely. The failure it risks is the one the project names elsewhere — a
      closed linguistic circuit that excludes the people with the most accurate
      knowledge of where a system fails. An analysis of administrative
      exclusion that requires a vocabulary nobody at the counter has received
      has built its own intake filter.
    </p>
    <p>
      The commitment that follows is not dilution. It is that every binding
      layer carries its meaning without the idiom: each standard page is
      readable on its own, the{" "}
      <a href="/explainers">plain-language explainers</a> and{" "}
      <a href="/explainers/language-people-can-use">copy-ready phrases</a>{" "}
      exist so the method can be used by someone who has never read the theory,
      and glossary anchors resolve to definitions written in sentences rather
      than in references to other sentences. Compression is allowed at the top
      of the stack and nowhere it blocks the bottom.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="reading-contract"
    eyebrow="Self-critique"
    title="The reading contract"
    description="What each layer asks of a reader, and what it may not ask."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <ul class="card__list">
      <li>
        A standard page is self-contained. Using STD-01 or the{" "}
        <a href="/standards/minimum-viable-contestability">
          minimum viable contestability standard
        </a>{" "}
        requires the page you are on and nothing else.
      </li>
      <li>
        Theory motivates and never gates. The{" "}
        <a href="/research/theory">essays</a> say why the laws hold; no
        instrument, validator, or checklist assumes you have read them.
      </li>
      <li>
        Every instrument states its inputs in plain terms. A diagnostic that
        cannot say what it asks for and what it returns in one screen is not
        done.
      </li>
      <li>
        Where the vocabulary is needed, it is linked at first use. A term that
        cannot be reached from where it appears is a defect, not a register of
        sophistication.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </SectionBlock>
  <CitationBlock
    title={pageTitle}
    permalink={permalink}
    publication={publication}
  />
</StandardsLayout>
